    {
      "id": "p0026-b005",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "Some sound-effect words, such as animal sounds, are generally easy to convert between languages. But some languages use sound-effect words more than others, and some languages have sound-effect words that don't even exist in other languages. For example, the Japanese language has a sound for staring. It even has a sound effect for silence. When it comes to sound effects, sometimes a literal translation isn't even possible.",
        "raw": "Some sound-effect words, such as animal sounds, are generally easy to convert\nbetween languages. But some languages use sound-effect words more than\nothers, and some languages have sound-effect words that don't even exist in\nother languages. For example, the Japanese language has a sound for staring. It\neven has a sound effect for silence. When it comes to sound effects, sometimes\na literal translation isn't even possible.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.28847605,
          0.26310714,
          0.89280446,
          0.36770714
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.28539609,
          0.26239286,
          0.89895428,
          0.36877857
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          10,
          11,
          12,
          13,
          14,
          15
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "动物叫声之类的拟声词，通常很容易在语言之间转换。但有的语言比别的语言更常用这类词，有的语言还有别的语言根本没有的拟声、拟态词。例如日语有表示盯着看的“声音”，甚至连寂静也有“声音”。碰到这类词，有时连直译都不可能。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": []
      },
