    {
      "id": "p0026-b009",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "Unlike English, the Japanese language has three distinct writing systems. This lets you spell the same word in multiple ways - sometimes dozens of ways - to achieve different effects. What's more, the Japanese language even allows you to write words on top of other words to achieve various effects not possible in other languages. Unsurprisingly, these effects tend to get flattened in a literal translation.",
        "raw": "Unlike English, the Japanese\nlanguage has three distinct writing\nsystems. This lets you spell the same\nword in multiple ways - sometimes\ndozens of ways - to achieve different\neffects. What's more, the Japanese\nlanguage even allows you to write\nwords on top of other words to\nachieve various effects not possible\nin other languages. Unsurprisingly,\nthese effects tend to get flattened in a\nliteral translation.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.28939499,
          0.53865,
          0.57637651,
          0.75409286
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.28539609,
          0.53793571,
          0.57768928,
          0.75516429
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          32,
          33,
          34,
          35,
          36,
          37,
          38,
          39,
          40,
          42,
          44,
          46
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "与英语不同，日语有三套不同的文字系统。同一个词可以有多种写法，有时甚至几十种，能产生不同效果。日语还可以在词语上面再写一层文字，做到其他语言做不到的事。这些效果往往在直译中被磨平，也就不奇怪了。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": []
      },
