    {
      "id": "p0026-b012",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "If you type \"58008\" on a calculator and turn it upside down, it makes English speaking schoolchildren laugh because it spells \"BOOBS\". This numberplay would make little sense to non-English speakers, however. Similarly, the Japanese language has its own unique forms of numberplay that make zero sense in other languages - November 4•h can be interpreted as \"Nice Butt Day\", for example. When language is involved, numbers lose their literal predictability.",
        "raw": "If you type \"58008\" on a calculator and turn it upside down, it makes English\nspeaking schoolchildren laugh because it spells \"BOOBS\". This numberplay\nwould make little sense to non-English speakers, however. Similarly, the\nJapanese language has its own unique forms of numberplay that make zero sense\nin other languages - November 4•h can be interpreted as \"Nice Butt Day\", for\nexample. When language is involved, numbers lose their literal predictability.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.28978882,
          0.79734286,
          0.89693463,
          0.90219286
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.28539609,
          0.79662857,
          0.89895428,
          0.90326429
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          49,
          50,
          51,
          52,
          53,
          54
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "在计算器上输入“58008”，再上下颠倒，会让英语国家的小学生发笑，因为它看起来像“BOOBS”（胸部）。不过，不懂英语的人恐怕不明白这个数字游戏。日语同样有别的语言完全看不懂的数字游戏，例如11月4日可以解作“美臀日”。一旦牵涉语言，数字也不再总能按字面理解。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": []
